        ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $16x+4y=12?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $16x+4y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16x+4y & =12 \\\\ 4y & =-16x+12 \\\\ y & =-4x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-4.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
